<PLUGIN name="&name;" Title="Logs Viewer" author="&author;" version="&version;" pluginURL="&selfURL;" launch="&launch;" icon="img/logsviewerplugin.png" min="7.2.0" support="https://github.com/Lazaros-Chalkidis/unraid-logsviewer/issues">

<DESCRIPTION>
Real-time system, Docker and VM log viewer with dashboard widget and dedicated Tools page - Log Backups and System Alerts. Live auto-refresh, severity badges, search, filtering, syntax highlighting and export.
</DESCRIPTION>

<CHANGES><![CDATA[

# Logs Viewer

## 2026.11.22

### New Features

- Date placeholders in custom log paths. Write `{YYYY}`, `{MM}` and `{DD}` in a path and they are replaced with today's date, so `/var/log/hosts/{YYYY}/{MM}/{DD}/nginx/access.log` always opens today's file. It works everywhere a custom log does: the widget, the Tools page, Alerts and Backups. Requested by shooftie.
- The Tools page has its own Automatic refresh, Autoscroll and Pause on hover, in Settings > Tool Page, separate from the widget's. With Automatic refresh off, a Refresh now button appears next to the download button. On a touch screen, touching the log holds it still and touching anywhere else lets it go.
- The settings page has a Guide link at the bottom, between Support and Credits. It opens the plugin's guide on GitHub.

### Fixed

- A very large log could slow the server down while it was open. The plugin counted every line of the file on each refresh. It now counts only what was added since the last refresh. A huge file is counted a bit at a time, so no single refresh has to read all of it, and until the count catches up the total shows the lines on screen.
- Backups were put together in RAM before being zipped, so a log of several GB could use up the server's memory. They are now put together inside the backup folder.
- A backup no longer runs while the array is stopped. It used to write into RAM in that case.
- When several alerts fired in the same scan, only the first one was sent as an Unraid notification. The others showed in the alert history but never reached you. Every alert is sent now, and Unraid lists them as Logs Viewer Alert, Logs Viewer Alert (2) and so on.
- The alert history was written to the flash drive every time an alert fired, which with a busy rule could be once a minute. It is kept in memory now and saved to the flash drive when the array stops, so it is still there after a normal shutdown or reboot. After a power cut the entries since the last stop are lost.
- Failed Login and Kernel Panic from Add Common Rules now wait 5 minutes before they fire again, they used to fire on every scan. Rules you already have are not changed.
- Pressing Apply could untick sources you had not touched. A system log, a container or a VM lost its tick if its log was empty at that moment, and with Docker or the VM service stopped, as they are while the array is stopped, every container and VM on the open tab was unticked. The same could happen when the list failed to load. A ticked source now stays ticked until you untick it, and the Docker and VM cards say when the service is not running.
- A custom log label, a user script name or a VM name with characters such as `<` or `&` was put into the settings page as HTML, so it showed wrong and could run as code. Names are shown as plain text now.
- On a tablet, tapping System, Docker or any other source button in the dashboard widget did nothing, so the log could not be changed. Touch screens of any size now get the same picker as phones.
- The settings page could show "Could not load sources (HTTP 403)", "Failed to load backups" or "Failed to load rules" until it was reloaded. Scan now ended in Error, a backup download failed and Merge on the Tools page came back empty. It happened once the page had been open for a while, sometimes within minutes. All of these now recover on their own.
- The state of a VM was often wrong: a running VM with a space in its name showed as stopped, and the Tools page labelled every VM that was shut off as Unknown. The dot and the label now follow the real state.
- Changes to alert rules were saved only if you pressed Apply while still on the Alerts tab. From any other tab they were dropped without a word. Apply now saves them from every tab, and the rules file on the flash drive is written only when a rule has changed.
- When the rule list failed to load, the editor still let you add a rule, and Apply then replaced your saved rules with that one. The editor now stays locked and offers Retry, and your rules are left as they were.
- An alert rule with Regex ticked never fired if its pattern had an escaped slash, such as `\/mnt\/user`. It works now, with or without the backslash. A regex that was not valid was saved without a word and never fired either. Apply now stops and names the rule.
- Docker alerts only read the normal output of containers that were running. Error output was skipped, and so was a container that had just stopped, so the lines it wrote as it crashed never reached a rule. Both are read now.
- Docker Crash from Add Common Rules looked for text that Docker itself reports, not for what a container writes, so it almost never fired. It now looks for what programs print when they crash. Rules you already have are not changed. To get the new one, remove your Docker Crash rule and press Add Common Rules again.
- The Dashboard widget kept asking the server for its log while the tile was collapsed or closed, and on a Dashboard opened in a background tab. It now stops, and refreshes the moment you open the tile again.
- Opening the Dashboard read every ticked log in full, and picking another source read its whole tab again. The widget now reads only the log on screen: one request when the Dashboard opens instead of one for every tab. A log you have not opened yet says Loading for a moment. The dots in a tab's list show the state from when the Dashboard was opened, and the dot of the source on screen keeps updating.
- With the tile collapsed, the row of sources in the header stayed on System whatever you had picked. It now follows the tab you are on.
- When the widget had to renew its access token, the lines that arrived with that refresh did not show until the log changed again.
- The backup set its folder to root-only every time it ran, and so did Apply. If Storage location pointed at a folder you share, everyone else lost access to it. A folder that already exists is now left as it is. Only a folder the backup creates is made root-only, and each backup file is saved readable by root alone, so backups still cannot be opened through a share. Apply no longer creates the folder, the first backup does. A folder that was locked before stays locked until you set its permissions back.
- A VM with brackets, `+`, `&` or non-Latin letters in its name could be ticked but never showed its log, and it was missing from backups. A user script with such a folder name was left out of the backup or saved under a different name. These names now work in the widget, on the Tools page and in backups. Inside a backup a script log is named after its folder as it is, so `my script` becomes `my script.log` and no longer `myscript.log`.
- Ticking a VM or a script with `<` in its name and pressing Apply could reset every Logs Viewer setting to its default. Names the settings file cannot hold are now greyed out as "unsupported name" and cannot be ticked: those with a comma, a double quote, `<`, `>`, a slash, a backslash, `${` or an HTML entity such as `&amp;`. Rename the VM or the script to use it.
- A custom log could vanish when you pressed Apply, without a word. It happened to a row whose label had no Latin letter or digit, to a row whose label matched another one apart from capitals and punctuation, and to a row with only a label or only a path. Apply now stops and says which row and why, and a label that cannot be used turns its row red as you type.
- A custom log with `İ` in its label could be ticked but never showed up, because the settings page and the server gave it different internal names. They now agree. Tick it once more after the update.
- A User Script whose name was not saved as UTF-8 text left the dashboard widget empty and the settings page without its lists of sources. Such a script is now listed under its folder name and everything else works.
- The Tools page and the settings page came up dark inside a light Unraid if Display Settings had never been saved on that server. They now read the theme the same way Unraid does.
- Every update left the previous package file on the flash drive, about 150 KB each time, until the plugin was removed. Older package files are now deleted once the new version is installed, and the update says which ones it removed. Files that a new version no longer ships are cleared from the running plugin at the update too, instead of staying until the next reboot.
- Pressing Apply wrote the settings and the custom log list to the flash drive every time, even when nothing had changed. Each file is now written only when its content is different.
- Picking 20s as the refresh interval of the dashboard widget saved 10s. It is saved as 20s now.
- With every system log unticked for the Tools page, the sidebar still listed Syslog, Dmesg, GraphQL API and Nginx Errors, and none of them opened. The System group is now left out and the page opens on the first source you have ticked.
- With every system log unticked for the dashboard widget, it opened on an empty System button even when containers, VMs, scripts or custom logs were ticked, and kept asking the server for that empty list on every refresh. It now opens on the first source there is, and the empty System button is hidden.
- On the Tools page, "Failed to fetch logs" stayed on screen after the connection came back, until the log got a new line. It now clears on the next refresh that works.
- A backup that included a container which had been removed, or that ran while Docker was stopped, saved a file holding only Docker's error message. On a second run the same day that file replaced the real log saved earlier. Such containers are now left out.
- When a backup ran a second time on the same day, the logs of sources that had been unticked in between stayed in that day's file. They are now taken out.
- On the dashboard widget, a counter and the filter it sets did not agree: Errors also listed every critical line, and a line with two levels showed up under both. A counter now shows exactly the lines it counts. The four numbers also stay as they are while a filter is on, where the others used to drop to zero.
- Lines marked `emerg` were not counted as critical on the widget, and lines marked `alert` were not counted on the Tools page. Both pages now use the same words for the four levels.
- After a change of filter the widget could report errors or criticals that were not new, such as "+4 err". It now reports only what arrived since the last refresh, whatever the filter.
- The "Selected" line of the widget and the chips of the collapsed tile showed internal names such as "Script:Nightly Rsync" or "custom:hosts-nginx". They now show the name each source has in its dropdown.
- Reset to Defaults left the ticked User Scripts and custom logs as they were, on the Dashboard, Tool and Backup tabs. It now unticks them like the other lists. The custom log paths themselves are kept.
- A hand-made request that sent a parameter or a setting as a list instead of text left warnings in the PHP log, could put a setting back to its default, and in some cases showed an error instead of the settings page. Such a parameter is now refused, and a setting sent that way keeps its saved value.
- When a log file was missing or could not be read, the message in the log panel showed the full path of the file. It now says only that the file was not found or is not readable.
- The Dashboard and the Tools page waited for Docker for as long as it took, so a Docker that did not answer held up the whole page. Docker and the VM service now get five seconds. After that the page comes up without the running state of the containers, and a container log that could not be fetched reads "Docker did not answer in time."
- The alert scanner could stay stuck on a container whose lines Docker did not hand over, and no other pass ran until that ended. Each container now gets 30 seconds and is skipped in that pass if it takes longer.
- System commands such as docker and virsh are now run by their full path, not looked up by name.
- On the Dashboard, a click on the refresh button, or a log picked from the list, at the moment the widget was renewing its access token was left waiting. The refresh button stayed disabled until the page was reloaded and the log stayed on "Loading…" until the next refresh.
- On the Tools page, one failed renewal of the access token left the log on "Failed to fetch logs (HTTP 403)" until the page was reloaded. The page now says so at once and tries again on every refresh.
- The backup storage location accepted any text. A path with a double quote, `<`, `>`, `${` or an HTML entity such as `&amp;` was read one way by the settings page and another way by the backup script, and some of them made the whole settings file unreadable. Such a path is now refused: Apply stops and says why. The backup script also refuses a location that uses `..` to leave `/mnt/user/`.
- **Scan now** right after a pass, or two passes close together, read the same container lines again and could raise the same alert twice. The scanner now remembers how far it has read in each container, as it already did for files. A container line is also no longer missed when two passes come further apart than the scan interval.
- When a log grew by more than a megabyte between two passes, the line lying across the megabyte was cut in two and a rule could miss it. The scanner now stops at the end of the last whole line.
- If a pass was stopped while it waited for Docker, the next one read the files again and sent the same notifications a second time. The scanner now saves how far it has read, and the alerts it has raised, as it goes.
- In the CSV export of the Tool page, a log line starting with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` could be run as a formula by the spreadsheet that opened the file. Such a line now gets an apostrophe in front.
- On the Tools page, coming back to the browser tab restarted the live refresh even in Merge and in pause. In Merge, the merged list was replaced by the live log of the last source as soon as that log got a new line. In pause, the log moved while the indicator still read "paused".
- In Alerts, the MITRE menu of a rule had no arrow in the dark themes, and in the light themes the arrow was repeated all over the field, on top of its text.
- On the Tools page, going back from Merge to Live showed the merged lines as the log of the selected source, and Export saved them under its name, until that log got a new line. Live now reads the selected log again.
- On the Dashboard, the login toast missed new logins while another System log, such as Dmesg or PHP Log, was on screen. It now follows them whatever log is shown.
- On the Tools page, a container or VM named `null`, `false` or `NaN` could not be opened, a VM named `0` was missing from the list, and a VM named like a list, such as `[1]`, opened the log of another VM or none.
- Backup cleanup deleted every old zip named after a day, such as `2020-01-01.zip`, even one the plugin had not made. It now looks inside and deletes only its own backups.
- An alert whose log line was long and not in Latin letters, or held a byte that is not UTF-8, was sent as a notification but never reached the alert history. Other alerts found in the same scan went missing from the history with it.
- Alerts from log files stopped while a User Script folder with a name that is not UTF-8 had a log of its own: the scanner could not save its place and skipped every new line. Container alerts kept working.
- On a phone, the bin of each row in Custom log paths sat alone on a third line, under the path. It is now on the first line, next to the label.
- In Settings, a dropdown whose saved value was not one of its options, for example after editing the cfg by hand, showed its first option, and Apply then saved that one. It now shows its default.
- Opening the backup calendar deleted any empty file named after a day, such as `2026-11-01.zip`, from the backup folder. The calendar now only reads the folder.
- Alerts broke while a log held a line with a NUL byte that matched a rule. A power cut can leave such a line, and so can a container that writes binary data. Every scan stopped at it: the alerts found before it could go out again on each scan, the logs after it were not read, and nothing reached the history. The scan now goes on and sends the line without the NUL bytes.
- A backup never finished when Docker stopped answering, and while it waited no other backup could start. A container whose log does not arrive within five minutes is now left out of that backup, and every container is left out when Docker does not answer within ten seconds.
- A file with the day's name that was not a zip archive, an empty one for example, stopped that day's backup without a word. The backup still leaves such a file alone, and a line in the syslog now says the backup was not written.
- After a burst of log lines the alert scanner fell behind: it read one megabyte of each log per pass, so alerts for anything written after the burst came as many passes late as the burst had megabytes. It now reads up to 16 megabytes of a log in one pass. A rule still fires once per log in a pass.
- The alert scanner took everything a container had written since the last scan into memory at once. A container that wrote a lot could push the scan over PHP's memory limit. The scan then stopped at that container, every time, and the containers after it were not checked. Container output is now read line by line, so a scan needs the same small amount of memory however much a container writes.
- On the Tools page, picking another source while the one before it was still refreshing left "Loading…" on screen until the next refresh, 3 to 20 seconds later, sometimes with "No data returned for this source." in between. A container and a VM with the same name could show each other's log until that refresh. The source you pick is now loaded at once, and a late answer for the one before it is ignored.
- On the Tools page, pressing Merge while the live log was refreshing could bring the live log back a moment later, drawn over the Merge view, or an error if that refresh failed. A late answer for the live log is now ignored once you are in Merge.
- Pressing Live right after picking a source in Merge, or right after pressing Merge with sources already picked, left a "Fetching 2 sources…" message where the live log should be, and hid the refresh indicator, until the log got a new line. A Merge that has not started yet is now cancelled when you go back to Live.
- In the widget, the word "emerg" was counted as critical but not coloured, and the JSON export wrote a line with "emerg" or "err" as "info". The word is now coloured, and the export writes those lines as emergency and error.
- When a filter matched no line, the widget's "No matching lines" message was counted as one line and its words "info" and "critical" were coloured like log levels. The line counter now shows 0 and the message is plain text.
- On the Tools page, after scrolling up the view stayed where it was even when the pointer left the log, and the only way back to the newest line was to scroll down. It now goes back to the newest line as soon as the pointer leaves. To keep the view where you put it, turn Autoscroll off in Settings > Tool Page.
- The Tools page could show a log up to 2 seconds late, depending on the refresh interval set for the widget. It now goes by its own interval.
- After the plugin was removed and installed again, scheduled backups and alerts did not run until you pressed Apply in the settings or restarted the server. The schedule is now loaded a moment after the install.
- Lines marked `crit`, as nginx writes them, were not counted as critical on the widget or on the Tools page, they got no colour, and the JSON export wrote them as "info". `crit` is now one of the Critical words.
- On the Tools page, changing the Merge selection while the sources were still loading could leave the earlier selection on screen: the bar read "3 sources merged" while the lines of the third were missing, until the selection changed again. The view now always shows the latest selection.
- On the Tools page, a refresh that was already on its way when the pointer entered the log still redrew it, so the text you were selecting was lost while the indicator read "paused". Nothing is redrawn now while the log is paused.
- On the Tools page, picking a source with the keyboard while the pointer rested on the log started the live refresh under the pointer, and the pause did not come back until you clicked in the log or moved the pointer out and in again. The new log is now loaded once and stays paused.
- A backup cleared its leftover work folders by name, so any folder or file named like `.staging-abc123` in the backup folder was deleted, even one the plugin had not made. It now deletes only work folders that carry its own mark.
- With Show toast set to Hide, the widget still asked the server for the syslog on every third refresh. It no longer does.
- A container name starting with a dash could get into the settings from a cfg edited by hand, and Docker read it as an option: `--help` put Docker's help text in place of the log, on screen and in the backup. A name must now start with a letter or a digit, as the names Docker gives always do.
- The settings page kept the tab of your last Apply in the settings file, so pressing Apply from another tab wrote to the flash drive even when nothing had changed. The tab is now kept in memory, and after a reboot the page opens on the Dashboard Widget tab.
- The Apply button of the settings page was always active, whether there was anything to save or not. It is now greyed out until something on the page differs from what is saved, and it greys out again if you put the value back.
- When Apply found something wrong, or a save, a mute or an unmute failed, the settings page said so in a pop-up of the browser. It now says it in a dialog of its own, in the look of the page. A very long name or path in the message is shortened in the middle.
- In Settings > Tool Page, the Refresh interval list did not show which choice is the default. It now reads "5 seconds - Default".
- The plugin keeps its working files in `/tmp/logsviewer_cache`, and it used that name without checking what was there. If another program on the server had made a folder or a link with that name first, the plugin's files, the access token of its pages among them, ended up in a place of that program's choosing. The plugin now uses the folder only when it is a real folder owned by root. When it is not, it writes nothing there, the log views read "Failed to fetch logs (HTTP 503)" and a line in the syslog says why.
- In the White and Azure themes, the thin lines between Support, Guide and Credits at the bottom of the settings page were invisible and the links lit up in the wrong colour. They now follow the theme.
- In the Clear alert history dialog, Cancel and Clear looked the same, both orange. Cancel is now plain and Clear is red, and the two sit in the middle of the dialog.
- The backup kept its lock in a file with a fixed name in `/tmp`. If another program on the server had put a link with that name there, the next backup emptied the file the link pointed to. The lock now lives in the plugin's own folder in `/tmp`, which the backup checks first. If that folder is not the plugin's, the backup does not run and a line in the syslog says why.
- In the Azure and Gray themes, most buttons of the settings page took Unraid's hover colour instead of their own. In Azure the OK of the dialogs could not be read while the pointer was on it. The buttons now keep the plugin's hover colours in every theme.
- On a phone the settings page sat at the left edge, with a gap on its right. It now fills the width.
- The Mute button on each row of the alert history looked like a stock Unraid button and was much bigger than the rest of the row. It is now a small button like the others on the settings page.

## 2026.10.08

### Fixed

- If you are updating from an older version, please reboot once afterwards. Older versions set up the backup and alert schedule the wrong way and overwrote root's own cron table in the process. User Scripts set to run hourly, daily, weekly or monthly stopped running, some system tasks ran twice, and the plugin's own schedule could vanish when another plugin saved its settings. It is done the way Unraid expects now and your schedule carries over, the reboot just puts root's cron table back. Reported by hummelm10.

## 2026.09.10

### Fixed

- The dashboard scrolled sideways into empty space when the widget sat in a narrow column. The invisible select behind the Filter button was being sized by Unraid rather than by the button, so it stuck out past the edge. Reported by astran0va.

## 2026.09.05

### New Features

- User Scripts logs. The plugin finds the scripts you have in the User Scripts plugin and lets you tick the ones you want to follow, separately for the dashboard widget, the Tools page, Backups and Alerts. Nothing to type in, scripts appear and disappear on their own, and the section says so if User Scripts is not installed. A log shows up once a script has run in the background or on a schedule.
- The Tools page now sits under a Viewers Suite entry in the Tools menu, shared with the other Viewers plugins.

### Fixed

- Settings dropdowns always showed the first option. Show only last N lines, font size, refresh interval and autoscroll were saved correctly, the box just never showed what you had picked.
- The first line of every log was missing. It only showed when the whole file fitted on screen, but it affected every source.
- A User Script running in the foreground now shows as running. It leaves no marker behind, so it used to look idle.
- The Filter menu on the Tools page had its options centred instead of lined up on the left.

### Improvements

- The Tools page uses the full width of the window and wastes less space at the bottom.
- The information notes across Settings now share one look instead of three.
- The category buttons in the dashboard widget wrap onto a second line on a narrow column instead of stretching it.
- Corrected a few setting descriptions that no longer matched what the setting does.
- Assorted spacing and sizing tweaks on the Tools page.

## 2026.08.01

### New Features

- Backup frequency: log backups can run every day, or every 2, 3 or 4 days. The setting sits next to the schedule time in Backup Logs, General.

### Fixed

- Updating the plugin no longer resets your settings. Backups and alerts were being switched off silently and stayed off until you opened Settings and pressed Apply.
- Uninstalling no longer deletes your log backups or your settings. It now prints where they were left so you can remove them yourself if you want to.
- Uninstalling stops the backup and alert jobs right away instead of at the next reboot.
- Uninstalling also clears the plugin's temporary files and any leftovers from older versions, so nothing of it stays behind apart from your settings and backups.
- Updating no longer leaves duplicate backup and alert schedules behind.
- Backup cleanup only removes its own backups. Other zip files in the same folder are left alone.
- The Warnings and Critical counters no longer show 0 on a long log that clearly has them.
- The Backup tab no longer fails to load its list on a server whose PHP has no zip support.
- Alert scans can no longer overlap. If a scan was still running when the next one started, or when you pressed Scan Now, the same log line could be reported twice.
- The source lists in Settings no longer stay stuck on "Failed to load sources" and "Scanning VMs...". They retry when you return to the tab, tell you what went wrong, and offer a Retry link.
- Scanning for sources no longer hangs when Docker or the VM service is slow. The rest of the list loads anyway.
- Custom logs show their real size instead of 0 B, and a file that is missing is marked as not found.
- Custom log paths typed without a leading slash are accepted.
- Saving an invalid custom log path now tells you, instead of dropping the row without a word.
- Pause on hover really pauses. The log is held still while the pointer is over it and catches up the moment you move away.
- Pause on hover also holds if new lines arrive mid refresh. The widget used to jump to the bottom and you lost your place.
- On the White and Azure themes, the Apply and Reset buttons, the footer links and the selected source in the Tools sidebar were too pale to read. They now use darker shades on those themes.

### Improvements

- Clicking an active severity badge again clears the filter, the same way the Tools page already worked.
- The severity badges also work when the Filter dropdown is switched off in settings.
- The dashboard widget stops refreshing while its browser tab is in the background and catches up when you switch back.
- Alerts no longer write to the USB flash on every scan, which is easier on the stick. After a reboot they start from what happens next instead of replaying old lines.
- Settings, alert rules and custom paths are written more safely, so a power cut cannot leave a half written file.
- Only the web font you picked is downloaded, and the font setting now says which options are web fonts and what happens without internet access.

## 2026.07.22

### Fixed

- Log lines containing angle brackets (like kernel tags or emails such as root@tower in brackets) no longer show as raw codes like &amp;lt; in the dashboard widget.
- Logs exported from the dashboard widget now contain the real characters instead of codes like &amp;#039; and &amp;lt;.

### Improvements

- Temporary cache files created by the plugin are now private to the system, so log content in them cannot be read by anything else on the server.
- Custom log paths now follow symbolic links before checking them, so a link can never point outside the allowed locations. This applies to viewing, alerts and backups.
- Hardened how the plugin embeds its settings into the Dashboard and Tool pages.

## 2026.07.21

### Fixed
- Support links now point to GitHub Issues, and the README wording was tightened

## 2026.06.06

This release is a big one. The Tool page has been completely rebuilt into a full log viewer, and there are new tools on the Alerts side plus a lot of polish across the plugin.

### Fixed

- Apostrophes and other characters in log lines no longer show as raw codes like &amp;#039;.
- The Tool page no longer shows an "HTTP 403" error after being left idle.
- Merge mode now orders lines from different sources correctly by time.
- On a fresh install, Dmesg no longer appears with a "log not found" error; empty (0 B) logs are now hidden from the very first load.
- Docker container log sizes are detected reliably, so the source pickers no longer grey out valid containers.
- The alert mute menu no longer gets cut off at the bottom of the list.
- The whole Tool page now fits on screen without page scrolling.

### New Features

- Rebuilt Tool page: a full-screen log viewer with a sidebar listing all your sources (System logs, Docker containers, VMs, and custom logs), grouped together with a status dot and the file size next to each one.
- Live tailing: the open log updates on its own. Pick the refresh rate (3, 5, 10 or 20 seconds) in settings. Hovering over the log pauses updates so lines do not scroll away while you are reading.
- Severity filtering: click the Info / Warnings / Errors / Critical pills to show only that level, and use the Filter dropdown for "and above" levels.
- Merge mode: pick two or more sources to see them combined in one stream, ordered by time.
- Search box in the footer to filter the current log as you type.
- Right-click a line to copy it or to filter the log on the selected text.
- Download button to save the current log as plain text, JSON or CSV (format is chosen in settings).
- Custom log paths: add any log file under /var/log, /mnt/user or /mnt/cache, and it becomes available everywhere (Tool page, widget, Alerts and Backup).
- Alerts: a Scan Now button for on-demand scans, the option to mute a rule for 1 hour / 24 hours / 7 days / forever, and tags on rules with a built-in MITRE ATT&CK technique picker.
- Log font size setting (Default or Large) for the Tool page.

### Improvements

- Settings and Tool pages now use the full width of large 2K and 4K monitors instead of leaving big empty margins.
- Tidier Settings footer with Support Forum, GitHub Issues and Credits links in one row.
- Stopped Docker containers and VMs now show a red dot, matching the dashboard widget.
- Sidebar dots now always reflect the source state and no longer change colour when you click a source.
- Alternating row shading in the log makes long lines easier to follow.
- Consistent "Docker Containers" wording across the whole plugin.
- Alert tracking now survives reboots and log rotation, so you do not get duplicate or missed alerts.
- One consistent button style across the Tool page.

## 2026.04.16

### Fixed
- Fixed file permissions (.page files 644, .sh files 755)

## 2026.04.15

### New Features
- Syslog Previous added as a selectable system log source in Dashboard and Tool page
- Three new theme presets: Midnight, Ocean and Monokai
- Log Backups: scheduled daily compressed backups of all enabled sources (system, Docker, VMs) with retention control, calendar view and one-click ZIP download
- Alert system: pattern-based rules that scan logs at configurable intervals and send notifications through Unraid's notification system, with built-in presets and alert history

## 2026.04.06

### Fixed
- Perf/syntax toast no longer gets stuck after using search, properly auto-dismisses after 6 seconds and does not reappear on the same log
- Hex color input was not accepting manual values
- Toast right side content was clipped on smaller widgets
- Theme preset selection (Terminal, Dim, Contrast) was not saving due to a mismatched validation whitelist
- Toast background no longer follows the log panel color when a theme preset is active, only when Default preset with a manual background color

### New Features
- Toast panel split into two slots: left side keeps existing messages (selected log, search, login, perf), right side shows contextual info
- File size displayed permanently on the right side of the toast bar
- New lines indicator: "+N new lines" in green when new lines are detected between polls
- Error spike indicator: "+N err" in orange when error count increases between polls
- Critical spike indicator: "+N crit" in red when critical count increases between polls
- Indicators persist until the next poll cycle brings new data
- "Paused" indicator appears on the right side of the toast when hovering over the log panel (requires pause on hover enabled)
- Toast background color in dark theme now follows the Log background color setting

### Improvements
- Default log background color changed to #1b1b1b
- Hex color input field now accepts manual typing (applied on blur or Enter, reverts if invalid)
- System Logs / Docker Logs / VM Logs sections in Settings redesigned to match the rest of the page (label column with checkbox and name on the left, path and size on the right, alternating row backgrounds, styled note and action rows)
- Toast separator lines made more visible
- File size added to all API responses with zero additional server overhead
- Settings page buttons (Apply, Reset to Defaults, Done) restyled to match StreamViewer design with color-coded variants (green for Apply, orange for Reset, neutral for Done)
- Log source action buttons (Select All, Deselect All, Scan) restyled to match the same design language

## 2026.03.31

### Bug Fixes
- Syntax dropdown not working on Unraid 7.2+ where tiles load after the page (switched to event delegation)
- HTML entities appearing literally in syntax highlighted output (added decode step before engine processing)
- Hover transition on buttons causing a diagonal fade out artifact (fixed across all stylesheets)
- Toast, hover backgrounds and toggle knob not adapting to Azure and White themes (replaced hardcoded colors with CSS variables)
- Log header highlight too faint on light theme backgrounds
- Critical badge staying dim when selected as active filter (added dedicated highlight class with proper specificity)
- Error badge color not matching the log level highlight used inside the log panel (changed to deep orange)
- Docker and VM dropdowns showing no running/stopped indicators (API was returning a generic placeholder instead of real states)
- Login toast shared between Dashboard and Tool pages (localStorage keys now prefixed with page context)
- Missing Syntax Engine and Show Toast entries in the reset to defaults maps for both Dashboard and Tool
- Section header accidentally placed inside a JS function body, breaking storageKey

### New Features
- Color coded proportion strip in the footer showing the ratio of info, warning, error and critical lines at a glance
- Colored category dots next to System (blue), Dockers (green) and VMs (orange) tab names
- Green or red status dot next to each Docker container and VM in dropdown menus based on running state
- Clicking a severity badge now activates that filter and highlights the badge so you can see which filter is active
- Five responsive breakpoints covering tablets down to small phones (1024, 768, 560, 480, 360px)
- Full support for all four Unraid themes: Black, Gray, Azure and White
- Single source polling: auto refresh fetches only the log you are viewing instead of every enabled source
- Content hash detection: when a log has not changed, the server returns a tiny "unchanged" response instead of the full payload
- Syntax highlighting libraries bundled locally, removing external CDN dependency
- VM log path protected against directory traversal with realpath validation
- Eight new whitelist validators for settings fields that previously accepted any input
- Security headers added to the cached API response path

### Improvements
- Redesigned footer layout with line count and pulse on the left, severity badges in the center, clock and timestamp on the right
- Severity badges restyled as colored pills with subtle borders and hover lift
- Syntax and Filter controls moved into the tabs rail as compact pill buttons
- Dropdown menus now fit their content width and have separator lines between items
- Clock icon replaced with a clean SVG matching the StreamViewer widget, timestamp switched to 24-hour format
- Default log font size changed from Medium to Large for better readability out of the box
- Critical badge color brightened for better visibility on dark backgrounds
- On tablets the header stacks vertically and the tabs row wraps; on phones the footer fully stacks and controls shrink
- Tool page goes full width on small screens with reduced padding
- Settings page compacts its form rows, fonts and buttons on narrow viewports
- Enable/Disable toggle thumb enlarged to fill the track properly
- Source action buttons sized to work through Unraid framework overrides
- Level highlighting regex compiled once at startup and collapsed from twelve passes into a single pass lookup
- Level counting, filter matching and tail trimming all optimized to avoid unnecessary string allocations
- Docker and VM state lookups cached per request so the same shell command never runs twice in one poll
- All htmlspecialchars calls switched to ENT_QUOTES so single quotes are escaped
- Control characters stripped from config values before writing the INI file
- Boolean toggle fields routed through the yes/no sanitizer
- Content hash parameter validated against a strict hex regex before comparison
- HTML sanitizer strips all span attributes except class and validates class values against a character whitelist
- Stylesheet reorganized into labeled sections with all responsive rules gathered at the end
- JavaScript reorganized into 18 labeled sections with a table of contents
- Every setting stored independently for Dashboard and Tool with separate config keys

## v2026.03.21

### New Features
- Theme Support: Full compatibility with Unraid Dark / White / Gray /Azure themes across Dashboard widget, Tool page, and Settings page

### Changes
- Prism syntax engine promoted from Beta to stable

## Version 2026.03.17

### Fixes
- Fixed widget stopping after ~1 hour (automatic CSRF token renewal)
- Reset to Defaults now works independently per tab (Dashboard and Tool no longer affect each other)
- Fixed syntax selection being lost when resetting the other tab
- Fixed panel height being reset on both pages when only one was reset
- Removed stray config key that could reset Dashboard syntax engine when resetting Tool

### Removed
- Removed "Refresh only when scripts are running" option (was ineffective and caused confusion)

## Version 2026.03.16

### Performance
- Fixed browser freezing during auto refresh on large logs (render skip when content unchanged)
- Docker logs now load in parallel instead of one by one (faster with multiple containers)
- Reduced unnecessary server requests during polling
- Faster line counting for large log files
- Improved server side response caching
- Google Fonts no longer block page loading
- Fixed incorrect mobile detection on low-core servers (Celeron, J series, i3)
- Removed redundant background timer

### Fixes
- Single quotes in logs now display correctly instead of showing as &#039;

## Version 2026.03.14

### Improvements
- Plugin: added Title display in Plugins page
- Plugin: minimum Unraid version updated to 7.2.0
- Security: support URL corrected to official forum thread
- Plugin: file permissions corrected (page files 644 instead of 755)

## Version 2026.03.07

### First release
- Dashboard Widget for real time log monitoring directly from the Unraid dashboard.
- Dedicated Tools page for full screen log viewing.
- Support for System logs: Syslog, Dmesg, GraphQL API, Nginx Errors, Libvirt.
- Support for Docker container logs with automatic container discovery.
- Support for VM logs with automatic VM discovery.
- Active log highlight in dropdowns, selected item is visually marked across all tabs.
- Search functionality with real time match counter and toast feedback.
- Log level filters: Info, Warnings, Errors, Critical with color coded badges.
- Status badges in footer showing live count of Info, Warnings, Errors and Critical entries.
- Optional syntax highlighting via Highlight.js or Prism (beta), two selectable engines.
- Performance safeguards for large logs: auto trim with "Large log" toast notification.
- Autoscroll toggle with smooth scroll to bottom behavior.
- Export log to file action (log, txt, json).
- Login toast notifications (success / failed) with IP detection.
- Tail lines presets: full log, 200, 500, 800, 1500 lines.
- Automatic refresh with configurable interval.
- Settings page with full configuration options.
- Dark theme with custom color support and CSS variable theming.
- Fully responsive layout compatible with all screen sizes, including mobile devices introduced in Unraid 7.2.x
- Resizable Widget: Adjustable height for the dashboard widget and tool
]]></CHANGES>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&pluginURL;</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
[ -f /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&name;-&version; ] || exit 0
STAMP=$(date +%Y-%m-%d,%T)
for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&name;-[0-9]*; do
  if [ ! -f "$pkg" ] || [ "${pkg##*/}" = "&name;-&version;" ]; then continue; fi
  mv "$pkg" "$pkg-upgraded-$STAMP" || continue
  removepkg "${pkg##*/}-upgraded-$STAMP" | grep -v 'Skipping\.\|Removing files:'
done
for txz in /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-[0-9]*.txz; do
  if [ ! -f "$txz" ] || [ "${txz##*/}" = "&name;-&version;.txz" ]; then continue; fi
  if rm -f "$txz"; then echo "Removed old package ${txz##*/}"; else echo "Could not remove old package ${txz##*/}"; fi
done
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
CFG=/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;.cfg
if [ ! -f "$CFG" ]; then
  mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/&name;
  printf '%s\n' 'REFRESH_ENABLED="1"' 'REFRESH_INTERVAL="10"' > "$CFG.tmp"
  mv "$CFG.tmp" "$CFG"
fi
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
chown -R root:root /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;
chmod -R 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;
find /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;
find /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; -name '*.sh' -exec chmod 755 {} \;

if [ -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-cron.conf ]; then
  mv /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-cron.conf /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer.cron
fi
rm -f /etc/cron.d/logsviewer-backup /etc/cron.d/logsviewer-alerts /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-backup.cron /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-alerts.cron

if [ -x /usr/local/sbin/update_cron ]; then
  /usr/local/sbin/update_cron
  LINK=/var/log/plugins/&name;.plg
  if [ ! -L "$LINK" ] &amp;&amp; [ -S /var/run/emhttpd.socket ] &amp;&amp; [ -s /boot/config/plugins/&name;/logsviewer.cron ]; then
    (
      for _ in {1..30}; do
        sleep 1
        if [ -L "$LINK" ]; then /usr/local/sbin/update_cron; break; fi
      done
    ) >/dev/null 2>&amp;1 &amp;
  fi
fi

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " &name; (&branch; build) has been installed."
echo " Version: &version;"
if crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'logsviewer-\|^# Generated'; then
  echo ""
  echo " Please reboot once when convenient. Older versions"
  echo " of this plugin changed root's cron table, so some"
  echo " tasks may run twice and hourly, daily, weekly or"
  echo " monthly User Scripts may not run. A reboot puts"
  echo " the original back."
fi
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
BPATH=$(grep '^BACKUP_STORAGE=' /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;.cfg 2>/dev/null | cut -d'"' -f2)

if [ -x /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-alerts.sh ]; then
  /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/logsviewer-alerts.sh flush
fi

for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&name;-[0-9]*; do
  if [ -f "$pkg" ]; then removepkg "${pkg##*/}"; fi
done
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-*.txz /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-*.txz.b64 /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-*.md5

rm -rf /tmp/logsviewer_cache /tmp/logsviewer-backup-*
rm -f /etc/cron.d/logsviewer-backup /etc/cron.d/logsviewer-alerts

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " &name; has been removed."
echo ""
echo " Kept on purpose, delete by hand if you want them gone:"
echo "   settings, alert rules and history:"
echo "     /boot/config/plugins/&name;"
if [ -d "$BPATH" ]; then
  echo "   log backups:"
  echo "     $BPATH"
fi
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
